From: Harvi654 <202698022+Harvi654@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] packager: Implement class, user type, save, extent, advise and IViewObject2 for packages.

AI-assisted (Claude), original code; LGPL-2.1-or-later. Not submitted
upstream.
Contributed by krokosik (Autodesk-Inventor-for-Linux issue #10, winum PR
#1), written with Claude and cross-reviewed by Codex
(docs/wine-repro/cross-review-2026-11-12-inventor-reference-model.md).

Windows (measured, Windows 10 22H2, 2026-11-11): the Package object created
for a file has IOleObject, IPersistStorage, IDataObject and IViewObject;
GetClassID/GetUserClassID return {0003000C-0000-0000-C000-000000000046},
GetUserType "Package", IsDirty S_FALSE, and OleSave writes \1CompObj and
\1Ole10Native; a saved package loads again.

Wine's packager could load a package but returned E_NOTIMPL for the class,
user type, IsDirty, Save, SaveCompleted, HandsOffStorage, GetExtent and
Advise, and had no IViewObject. Autodesk Inventor 2027 asks for each of
these (in this order: IViewObject2, Advise, SetHostNames, GetUserClassID,
then GetClassID/Save on save) and drops the object when IViewObject2 or
Advise fails (SetHostNames may fail).

Save writes back the \1Ole10Native stream as loaded (Windows rewrites it
with the path of its own temporary copy). IDataObject is not implemented;
no caller here asks for it (Windows' Package answers GetData with
CF_METAFILEPICT and CF_ENHMETAFILE). Needs ole32 (OleRegGetUserType,
CreateOleAdviseHolder, WriteClassStg, WriteFmtUserTypeStg) and gdi32 in
IMPORTS.

v2 (2026-11-11, after cross review): InitNew stays E_NOTIMPL (not
measured, not needed); Load checks the stored path lengths against its
MAX_PATH buffers and drops the kept stream when it fails. Header
corrected: SetHostNames still returns E_NOTIMPL and Inventor continues;
the icon extent (96 dpi) and EnumAdvise with no sinks are not measured.

v3 (2026-11-12, after cross review by Codex; measured with pkgprobe,
Windows 10 22H2 x64 and x86, ole32 docs/wine-repro/ole32-createfromfile-html):
- Load keeps the stream in a temporary docfile instead of memory (no 1 GiB
  limit), copying with Read/Write only (the container's stream may not
  implement CopyTo); a file name that does not fit into the temporary
  directory with a " (n)" suffix fails with E_FAIL, as OleLoad does on
  Windows, instead of overflowing the buffer.
- Save writes the class and \1CompObj ("OLE Package") itself, as
  IPersistStorage::Save does on Windows.
- EnumAdvise: E_FAIL before the first Advise, then E_NOTIMPL with a NULL
  enumerator (measured).
- Extent and Draw: the same for every aspect and index (measured).
- SetAdvise sends no notification for ADVF_PRIMEFIRST, as measured; no
  change needed.

v4 (2026-11-12, after the second review; measured with pkgprobe on
Windows 10 22H2, x64 and x86, ole32 docs/wine-repro/ole32-createfromfile-html):
- Temporary copy: the name itself must fit (GetTempPath + name + 1 <= 260),
  and a numbered name only when the first is taken; else E_FAIL. This
  matches Windows at every length measured (251 to 261, with and without
  a collision); v3 refused names that fit.
- Extent: Windows' size, in screen pixels: width 5/4 of the wider of the
  icon and the label plus one, height the icon plus 11/10 of the label's
  height plus 5.5, label in the icon title font; inferred from, and exact
  for, every case measured (three names, icon title fonts of 15 and 25
  pixels). The height
  rests on these two font sizes; the font cannot be changed for a
  measurement outside an interactive desktop.
- Draw keeps the caller's mapping: the bounds are mapped to device pixels
  first, so moving the DC's origin moves the drawing by as much (as on
  Windows); the DC is left as it was. Windows' exact pixel placement
  inside the bounds is not reproduced.
- Load reads a package whose ANSI path is longer than its buffer through
  the UTF-16 path.
- Tests: the sink returned by GetAdvise is released.

v5 (2026-11-12, after the third review): the name decoded from the ANSI
path was counted with its terminator, so a package without the UTF-16
names was refused one character too early; trailing terminators are
dropped before the length check (Windows: the same limit with and without
the UTF-16 names, measured). The extent rule is an inference from the
measured cases (two icon title font sizes), stated as such.

